+# ::a1 Explanatory Example Debate
+
+::a2 This document is a _debate_. ::a3 Its purpose is to describe and motivate the main features of _Fair Debate_ — the platform you are looking at. ::a4 Please be aware that this document kind of abuses the platform's functionality (i.e. the special way to display a stream of speech and counter-speech) for explanations. ::a5 Actually Fair Debate is not meant for displaying documentation but as tool for constructive and reliable communication about possibly controversial topics.
+
+::a6 You are reading the opening _contribution_ of this debate. ::a7 It has the contribution key `a`. ::a8 Every contribution is split automatically into _segments_ — headings, sentences, bullet points. ::a9 The segments of a contribution carry keys of their own like `a1`, `a2`, etc. ::a10 Therefore each segment can be precisely referenced. ::a11 And each segment can be answered on its own. ::a12 Hovering over a segment shows its key; clicking or tapping it also lets you copy its URL and answer it.
+
+::a13 Top right of the header sits an ![info](/static/img/info-icon.svg) symbol, an `i` in a circle. ::a14 It switches on help mode: ::a15 from then on the interface explains its own controls. ::a16 Click an element to see its explanation, or step through all of them in turn with the arrows. ::a17 The key `i` switches the mode on and off as well.
+
+::a18 Segments that are highlighted have been answered already. ::a19 Click one such answered segment to unfold the answers below it. ::a20 Most of this document lives in those answers, so unfolding them is how you read it.
+
+::a21 Multiple parties (users) can participate in a debate. ::a22 Parties are identified by letters, assigned in the order in which they join a debate.
+
+- ::a23 `a` is the party that opens the debate, which here is me.
+- ::a24 `b` is the first party to answer.
+- ::a25 `c` is the second party to answer.
+- ::a26 Further parties continue the alphabet and beyond (see below).
+
+::a27 A party may also answer a segment of its own. ::a28 That is how a later addition can be introduced to the debate instead of rewriting what was already published.
+
+::a29 Two main features set this platform apart from an ordinary comment section or forum. ::a30 They are explained in the answers.
+
+1. ::a31 Persistent in-context answers.
+2. ::a32 Provable integrity of the content data.
+
+::a33 If you have understood those main features you can use Fair Debate pretty well. ::a34 However, there are some more details you might be interested in.
+
+::a35 The format alone, though, does not make a debate a good one. ::a36 Everybody who writes here commits themselves to six rules — among them keeping in mind that one may be wrong, separating description from judgement, and correcting one's own mistakes. ::a37 The full text is published as the _self-commitment to a constructive debate culture_ at [/rules](/rules/).
+
+## ::a38 FAQ
+
+::a39 Each of these is answered below, in an answer of its own — which is at the same time a small demonstration of what this platform does.
+
+- ::a40 What happens if more parties join the debate than there are letters in the alphabet?
+- ::a41 Is it possible to answer multiple segments or parts of segments?
+- ::a42 How can the integrity of a debate (i.e. the absence of manipulation) be checked?
+- ::a43 What do the signature and the fingerprint each achieve?
+- ::a44 How is the platform moderated?
+- ::a45 How does the project collect feedback?
+- ::a46 Is it possible to help the project?
